  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>(A) Young&#39;s modulus is stated as<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Stress</mi><mi>Strain</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mrow><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>l</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#8710;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi>MLT</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfenced><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></mfenced></mrow><mrow><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfenced><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></mfenced></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mi>ML</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math>(B) Coefficient of viscosity is stated as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#951;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi></math>&nbsp;is velocity.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>&#951;</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi>MLT</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfenced><mrow><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi></mfenced><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi>LT</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfenced></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mi>ML</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>(C) Planck&#39;s Constant is stated as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>Energy</mtext><mtext>Frequency</mtext></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mi>ML</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfenced></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mi>ML</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>(D) Work function has the same dimension as that of energy, so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mi>&#981;</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfenced open=\"[\" close=\"]\"><mrow><msup><mi>ML</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Thus,&nbsp;A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV</p>",
